<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Right Triangle Area From Altitude</h2>
  <p>Suppose a right triangle has a hypotenuse of 10 m, and the perpendicular distance from the right-angle corner to that hypotenuse measures 4.8 m. Its area follows directly: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> The triangle encloses 24 square metres of flat surface.</p>
  <p>This calculator exists for exactly that situation. When you know the longest side of a right triangle, the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and the perpendicular altitude from the right-angle vertex to that side, <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />, the two legs are not needed. The hypotenuse serves as the base, the altitude is its matching perpendicular height, and every triangle's area is half the product of a base and its corresponding height: <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude must meet the hypotenuse at a right angle; a slanted segment that is not perpendicular gives a wrong height and a wrong area. Enter both measurements in matching length units, and the calculator returns the area in the corresponding square units, with each substitution step shown.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the geometry -->
<section id="sec-understand" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Hypotenuse and Altitude of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>This guide uses one consistent notation throughout: <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> are the two acute angles, <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> is the right angle, side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> lies opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> lies opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is the perpendicular altitude from vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> to the hypotenuse meeting it at point <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q]]')} /> are the hypotenuse segments <span set:html={rich('[[AD]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[DB]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area.</p>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle contains exactly one 90-degree angle. Its two legs meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse lies opposite it. The hypotenuse is always the longest side. The other two angles are acute, and because the three angles sum to 180 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]].')} /> These facts matter because the altitude method leans on them: the base must be the hypotenuse, and the height must drop from the right-angle vertex.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, labelled <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> in the calculator. To identify it in any orientation, find the side that does not touch the square corner marker: that side is the hypotenuse. In the altitude formula it plays the role of the base, so recognizing it correctly is the first skill this page teaches.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Altitude?</h3>
  <p>An altitude is a perpendicular segment from a triangle's vertex to the line containing the opposite side. Here the altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> runs from the right-angle vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> to the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[AB]]')} /> and meets it at point <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[CD\\perp AB]].')} /> It is a perpendicular distance, not simply any line drawn inside the triangle: only the segment that meets the hypotenuse at 90 degrees qualifies.</p>

  <h3>Is the Altitude the Same as a Leg?</h3>
  <p>No. The legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> meet at the original right angle, while the altitude is a separate segment from that same vertex to the opposite side. Take a 6-8-10 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]].')} /> Its altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h=(6\\times8)/10=4.8]].')} /> Neither leg measures 4.8, yet the altitude to the hypotenuse is exactly 4.8. Confusing a leg with the altitude is one of the most common errors on this page.</p>

  <h3>Why the Altitude Falls Inside the Triangle</h3>
  <p>For a non-degenerate right triangle, the perpendicular from the right-angle vertex lands on the hypotenuse between its endpoints, never outside it. It splits the original triangle into two smaller right triangles. Those two triangles share the acute-angle measures of the original, which makes all three triangles similar to one another. That similarity is the reason the altitude satisfies such tidy relationships, explored in the geometric mean section below.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with legs a and b, hypotenuse c as base, altitude h from vertex C to point D, formula K = ch/2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Anatomy of the altitude method: the hypotenuse c is the base, the altitude h meets it at 90 degrees at point D, and the area is K = ch/2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: The formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Area From Altitude Formula</h2>
  <p>The altitude area formula is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ch}{2}}]]]')} /> Here <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse serving as the base, and <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is the perpendicular altitude to the hypotenuse. Three symbols, one multiplication, one halving.</p>

  <h3>Why Do We Multiply the Hypotenuse by the Altitude?</h3>
  <p>The general triangle area formula is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{\\text{base}\\times\\text{height}}{2}]]')} />, and the base and height must be perpendicular to each other. Choosing the hypotenuse as the base forces the height to be the perpendicular distance to it, which is exactly the altitude: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{base}=c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{height}=h]]')} />, therefore <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]].')} /> This holds however the triangle is rotated or drawn: perpendicularity is a geometric relationship, not a screen direction, so a slanted hypotenuse works as a base just as well as a horizontal one.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Result Divided by Two?</h3>
  <p>A triangle occupies half of the parallelogram built on the same base with the same perpendicular height. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8]]')} />, the product <span set:html={rich('[[c\\times h=48]]')} /> is the area of that parallelogram. The triangle is half of it, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=48/2=24]].')} /> Reporting 48 would answer a different question: the area of the full parallelogram, not the triangle inside it.</p>

  <h3>Does This Formula Work for Every Triangle?</h3>
  <p>The base-height rule works for every triangle, provided the height is perpendicular to the chosen base. What is special here is the choice of base: the hypotenuse of a right triangle, with the altitude dropped from the right-angle vertex. That choice brings extra geometric constraints, such as the altitude never exceeding half the hypotenuse, which do not apply to general triangles.</p>

  <h3>Why the Formula Does Not Need the Two Legs</h3>
  <p>Area needs one side and its corresponding perpendicular height, nothing more. When <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> are already known, the legs add no new information about the area. The legs formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> is simply a different input route to the same quantity, useful when the measured data are the two perpendicular sides instead.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-area-half.svg')} alt="Why triangle area is half of base times height: c = 10, h = 4.8, 10 x 4.8 = 48, divided by 2 gives 24 square units" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The product c times h is the full rectangle; the triangle fills exactly half of it, which is why the formula divides by two.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Step-by-step example -->
<section id="sec-steps" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Calculating Area From Altitude</h2>
  <p>One complete calculation, from measurements to verified answer, using <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8\\text{ m}]]')} /></p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the given measurements.</strong> The hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} /> and the perpendicular altitude to it is <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8\\text{ m}]].')} /> Confirm the altitude meets the hypotenuse at a right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Write the formula.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]].')} /></li>
    <li><strong>Substitute the measurements.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]].')} /></li>
    <li><strong>Multiply.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[10\\times4.8=48]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=48/2]].')} /></li>
    <li><strong>Divide by two.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /></li>
    <li><strong>Interpret the answer.</strong> The triangle encloses 24 square metres of flat area.</li>
    <li><strong>Check feasibility.</strong> A valid right triangle needs <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]]')} />; here <span set:html={rich('[[4.8\\leq5]]')} />, so the measurements can describe a real right triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify using the legs.</strong> A 6-8-10 triangle has the same hypotenuse and altitude, and <span set:html={rich('[[K=(6\\times8)/2=24]].')} /> Both routes agree.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area From Altitude Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page needs exactly two inputs. The walkthrough below follows the on-screen order, so a first-time user can go from a drawn or measured triangle to a verified area.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the right triangle.</strong> Confirm one angle is 90 degrees; the method only applies to right triangles.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate its hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]].')} /></strong> It is the side opposite the right angle and the longest side.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the perpendicular altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]].')} /></strong> It runs from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse and meets it at 90 degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure or obtain both values.</strong> Measure directly, or compute the altitude first with <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} /> when the legs are known.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse into the Hypotenuse c field.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Enter the altitude into the Altitude h field.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible length units.</strong> Both inputs must use the same unit before the formula is applied.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm both values are positive.</strong> Zero or negative lengths describe no triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is no greater than half the hypotenuse.</strong> For <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, any altitude above 5 cannot belong to a right triangle with that hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Review the area result.</strong> It appears in square units matching the input unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the displayed solution steps.</strong> They show the formula, the substitution, and the arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Inspect the live visualization.</strong> Confirm the diagram's hypotenuse and altitude match the intended measurements.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset for a new calculation.</strong> It clears both fields so the next calculation starts clean.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator does not ask for the two legs or an angle, because the altitude method genuinely does not need them.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Four foundational examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Right Triangle Area From Hypotenuse and Altitude</h2>
  <p>Four worked examples build the method from familiar triangles upward. Each one computes the altitude from the legs where needed, applies <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />, and cross-checks against the legs formula.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[h=(3\\times4)/5=2.4]].')} /> The area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times2.4)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} /> Checking with the legs, <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times4)/2=6]].')} /> Both formulas describe the same triangle, so they must agree, and they do.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]].')} /> The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h=(5\\times12)/13=60/13\\approx4.61538]].')} /> Keeping the exact fraction, <span set:html={rich('[[K=(13\\times(60/13))/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ square units}}]]]')} /> Retaining <span set:html={rich('[[60/13]]')} /> instead of a rounded decimal avoids an early-rounding error: the 13 cancels exactly, leaving a clean 30.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A 6-8-10 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8]]')} /> directly. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ square units}}]]]')} /> With legs 6 and 8, <span set:html={rich('[[K=(6\\times8)/2=24]]')} />, which confirms the altitude route.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: An Isosceles Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=5]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times5)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=25\\text{ square units}}]]]')} /> This is the largest area any right triangle with hypotenuse 10 can have. The altitude equals half the hypotenuse only when the legs are equal, making each acute angle <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />, and no valid right triangle with this hypotenuse can exceed 25 square units.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: Practical applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Right Triangle Area From Altitude</h2>
  <p>Twelve worked situations show the formula at different scales and in different units. Each names the triangle in the scene, the two measurements, the substitution, the area, and the assumption behind the model.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Triangular Garden Section</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular garden section has a longest boundary of 10 m, and the perpendicular distance from the right-angle corner to that boundary is 4.8 m. So <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> The section occupies 24 square metres. This assumes the boundary is straight and the corner is a true right angle.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Triangular Plywood Panel</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular plywood panel has a hypotenuse of 1 m and a perpendicular altitude of 0.48 m: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(1\\times0.48)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=0.24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> That is the flat surface of one face, useful for coverage estimates. Cutting waste and material allowances are separate considerations.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Right-Triangular Glass Window</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular glass panel has a hypotenuse of 1.3 m and an altitude of 0.6 m perpendicular to that side: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(1.3\\times0.6)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=0.39\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> This is the geometric glass area before frame coverage or installation allowances are considered.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: Surveying a Triangular Section</h3>
  <p>A survey drawing contains a right triangle with hypotenuse 13 m and altitude <span set:html={rich('[[60/13]]')} /> m. Keeping the fraction: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(13\\times(60/13))/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> The drawing represents a 30-square-metre section. Actual field work may still need instrument corrections beyond the plan.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Measuring a Triangular Tile</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular tile has a hypotenuse of 25 cm and a perpendicular altitude of 6.72 cm: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(25\\times6.72)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=84\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} /> One tile covers 84 square centimetres, which helps estimate how many tiles a surface needs. Grout gaps and cutting losses are counted separately.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Steel Support Panel</h3>
  <p>A flat right-triangular support panel has a hypotenuse of 1.7 m with legs of 0.8 m and 1.5 m. Its altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h=(0.8\\times1.5)/1.7=12/17]]')} /> m. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(1.7\\times(12/17))/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=0.6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> That is the geometric panel area, not its load-bearing capacity.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Triangular Ramp Side Panel</h3>
  <p>A simplified ramp side panel forms a right triangle with a hypotenuse of 5 m and a perpendicular altitude of 2.4 m to that hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times2.4)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> This is the flat area of the triangular side section, not the sloping ramp surface, and the calculation does not establish accessibility compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Roof Gable Section</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular roof gable section has a hypotenuse of 6.5 m and a perpendicular altitude of <span set:html={rich('[[30/13]]')} /> m: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(6.5\\times(30/13))/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=7.5\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> The result describes the triangular plane given by the measurements. A real roof's total surface area is a separate three-dimensional measurement problem.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Right-Triangular Land Plot</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular land section has a hypotenuse of 50 m and a perpendicular altitude of 24 m: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(50\\times24)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=600\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> The measured boundaries enclose 600 square metres. An official area figure may still require survey verification.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Large Triangular Survey Plot</h3>
  <p>A surveyed right-triangular section has a hypotenuse of 100 m and a perpendicular altitude of 48 m: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(100\\times48)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=2400\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} /> That is the planar area of the triangular section. It represents the whole property only when the property consists solely of that triangle.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Triangular Sail Section</h3>
