<!-- Section 8: Why both formulas agree -->
<section id="sec-equal" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Both Right Triangle Area Formulas Give the Same Answer</h2>
  <p>A right triangle can be measured through its two perpendicular legs or through its hypotenuse and altitude. The legs formula is <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> and the altitude formula is <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]].')} /> Since both describe the same triangle's area, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{ab}{2}=\\frac{ch}{2}]].')} /> Multiply both sides by two: <span set:html={rich('[[ab=ch]].')} /> Solve for <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=\\frac{ab}{c}}]]]')} /> The altitude is the product of the legs divided by the hypotenuse, which is why the two formulas can never disagree on a consistent triangle.</p>
  <p>Numerical verification: for <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, the altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h=(6\\times8)/10=4.8]].')} /> By legs: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(6\\times8)/2=24]].')} /> By altitude: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2=24]].')} /> The same triangle, the same area, two routes.</p>
  <p>When only the hypotenuse and altitude are known, the area is already fixed: there is no need to reconstruct the legs first. The leg lengths can still be recovered from valid <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> through the segment relationships below, but that is a separate computation from finding the area. When the legs are the known data instead, the <a href="/right-triangle-area-from-legs-calculator/">Right Triangle Area From Legs Calculator</a> takes them directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: Geometric mean -->
<section id="sec-geomean" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Altitude Divides a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The altitude meets the hypotenuse at <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} />, splitting it into the segments <span set:html={rich('[[AD=p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[DB=q]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[p+q=c]].')} /> That split creates two smaller right triangles nested inside the original, and their proportions are remarkably tidy.</p>

  <h3>Two Smaller Similar Right Triangles</h3>
  <p>The altitude divides the original triangle into two smaller right triangles, each sharing one acute angle with the original and each containing a right angle. Sharing two angle measures makes all three triangles similar. Similarity is what forces the clean proportional relationships between the altitude, the segments, and the legs.</p>

  <h3>The Altitude Geometric Mean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Similarity gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h^2=pq}]]]')} />, or equivalently <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=\\sqrt{pq}}]]]')} /> The altitude is the geometric mean of the two hypotenuse segments: its square equals their product. Worked example: with <span set:html={rich('[[p=6.4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=3.6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=p+q=10]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{6.4\\times3.6}=\\sqrt{23.04}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=4.8}]]]')} /> The area follows: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ square units}}]]]')} /> The segment theorem verifies an altitude measured or computed another way.</p>

  <h3>Relationship Between the Segments and Legs</h3>
  <p>The same similarity yields <span set:html={rich('[[b^2=cp]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a^2=cq]].')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[p=6.4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[q=3.6]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{10\\times6.4}=\\sqrt{64}=8]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{10\\times3.6}=\\sqrt{36}=6]].')} /> The original 6-8-10 triangle is recovered exactly, which shows how tightly the segments, the altitude, and the legs are bound together.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Maximum altitude -->
<section id="sec-max" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Large Can the Altitude of a Right Triangle Be?</h2>
  <p>Not every pair of positive numbers can be a hypotenuse and altitude. For a valid, non-degenerate right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{0<h\\leq\\frac{c}{2}}]]]')} /> The altitude must be positive and can never exceed half the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>Why the Altitude Cannot Exceed Half the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>From <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=pq]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[p+q=c]]')} />, the product <span set:html={rich('[[pq]]')} /> is largest when the two segments are equal, <span set:html={rich('[[p=q=c/2]].')} /> Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[h^2\\leq c^2/4]]')} />, and for positive lengths <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]].')} /> In plain terms: the altitude is tallest when it splits the hypotenuse into two equal halves, and even then it reaches only half the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>Maximum Area for a Fixed Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]]')} />, the largest possible area is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_{\\max}=\\frac{c^2}{4}}]]]')} /> This maximum belongs to the isosceles right triangle, where the two legs are equal. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, the tallest valid altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h_{\\max}=5]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[K_{\\max}=(10\\times5)/2=25]].')} /> An altitude of 6 with a hypotenuse of 10 is impossible for a right triangle.</p>

  <h3>What Happens If the Altitude Is Too Large?</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=6]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[6>5]]')} />, so no right triangle has these measurements. The arithmetic <span set:html={rich('[[(10\\times6)/2=30]]')} /> is a valid area for some triangle in a more general setting, but it does not make <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[h=6]]')} /> a valid right-triangle input. This page does not claim the calculator rejects such input automatically; the check is yours to run.</p>

  <h3>Can the Altitude Equal Half the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Equality gives the isosceles right triangle with two equal legs, a valid non-degenerate triangle. It is the boundary case, not a broken one.</p>

