</section>

<!-- Section 11: Finding the altitude -->
<section id="sec-find-altitude" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Altitude When It Is Unknown</h2>
  <p>This calculator takes <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> as an input, so an unknown altitude must be found or measured first. Three standard routes cover the common cases. When the sides are known and the altitude is the missing piece, the <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-sides-calculator/">Right Triangle Altitude From Sides Calculator</a> computes it directly.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Using Both Legs and Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=\\frac{ab}{c}}]]]')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[h=(6\\times8)/10=4.8]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Method 2: Using Hypotenuse Segments</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=\\sqrt{pq}}]]]')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[p=6.4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=3.6]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{23.04}=4.8]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Method 3: Using Known Area and Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Rearranging <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=\\frac{2K}{c}}]]]')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[K=24]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[h=(2\\times24)/10=4.8]]')} /></p>

  <p>These are mathematical rearrangements, not extra calculator modes: the area-from-altitude calculator itself still expects the altitude as an input.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Units -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Square Units in Right Triangle Area Calculations</h2>
  <p>Area is two-dimensional. Multiplying two lengths multiplies their units too: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{m}\\times\\text{m}=\\text{m}^2]].')} /> The area unit is always the square of the length unit.</p>

  <h3>Square Metres</h3>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=24\\text{ m}^2]].')} /> Read it as 24 square metres of surface.</p>

  <h3>Square Centimetres</h3>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[c=25\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=6.72\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=84\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Square Millimetres</h3>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[c=250\\text{ mm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=120\\text{ mm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=15000\\text{ mm}^2]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Converting Area Units</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[1\\text{ m}^2=10000\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1\\text{ cm}^2=100\\text{ mm}^2]].')} /> A linear conversion multiplies by 10, 100, or 1000, but an area conversion squares that factor: 100 cm in a metre becomes <span set:html={rich('[[100^2=10000]]')} /> square centimetres in a square metre. Converting the length and converting the area are different operations.</p>

  <h3>What If the Inputs Use Different Units?</h3>
  <p>Convert first, calculate second. A hypotenuse in metres and an altitude in centimetres must be brought to one unit, for example 2 m and 150 cm become 2 m and 1.5 m, before the formula is applied. Mixed units in the formula produce a meaningless number with no clean unit.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Using the Altitude Area Formula</h2>
  <p>Most wrong answers on this page come from mislabelled inputs, not bad arithmetic. The eighteen mistakes below cover nearly every one.</p>

  <h3>1. Using a Leg Instead of the Altitude</h3>
  <p>The altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> drops from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse; a leg runs along the triangle's edge. In a 6-8-10 triangle, entering leg 6 as the altitude gives <span set:html={rich('[[(10\\times6)/2=30]]')} /> instead of 24. Fix: the altitude must land on the hypotenuse with a right-angle marker.</p>

  <h3>2. Confusing the Altitude With the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Swapping <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> changes the product's meaning. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8]]')} />, the numbers commute in multiplication, but the feasibility check <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]]')} /> breaks: the swapped entry claims an altitude of 10 on a hypotenuse of 4.8, which is impossible. Fix: the hypotenuse is always the longest side.</p>

  <h3>3. Drawing the Altitude From the Wrong Vertex</h3>
  <p>Only the altitude from the right-angle vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> pairs with the hypotenuse as its base. A perpendicular from an acute vertex lands on a leg, not the hypotenuse, and does not belong in this formula. Fix: start at the square corner.</p>

  <h3>4. Measuring the Altitude at a Non-Perpendicular Angle</h3>
  <p>Any non-perpendicular segment from <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> to the hypotenuse is longer than the true altitude, so the area comes out too large. Fix: verify the 90-degree meeting, by construction or with <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} /></p>

  <h3>5. Using a Height That Matches a Different Base</h3>
  <p>The altitude to the hypotenuse pairs only with the hypotenuse. Pairing it with a leg, as in <span set:html={rich('[[(8\\times4.8)/2=19.2]]')} />, mixes two different base-height pairs. Fix: each height belongs to exactly one base.</p>

  <h3>6. Forgetting to Divide by Two</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[10\\times4.8=48]]')} /> is the parallelogram's area, not the triangle's. Fix: end every base-height calculation with the halving.</p>

  <h3>7. Multiplying Lengths With Incompatible Units</h3>
  <p>A hypotenuse of 2 m with an altitude of 150 cm is not <span set:html={rich('[[(2\\times150)/2=150]]')} /> of anything meaningful. Fix: convert to one unit first, here 2 m and 1.5 m, giving 1.5 m².</p>

  <h3>8. Reporting the Answer in Linear Units</h3>
  <p>An area of 24 m² is not 24 m. Fix: always write the square unit with the number.</p>

  <h3>9. Entering Zero or Negative Lengths</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[c>0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h>0]]')} /> are required; zero or negative inputs describe no triangle. Fix: measure again.</p>

  <h3>10. Entering an Altitude Greater Than Half the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[h=6]]')} /> violates <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]].')} /> Fix: recheck which segment was measured; it was not the perpendicular altitude.</p>

  <h3>11. Assuming Every Positive Pair Forms a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Positivity is not enough; the altitude limit must also hold. Fix: run the <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]]')} /> check on every new pair.</p>

  <h3>12. Confusing the Altitude With the Vertical Leg</h3>
  <p>In a diagram the altitude may look vertical while a leg looks slanted, or the reverse after rotation. Vertical on screen means nothing. Fix: identify sides by their geometric role, not their screen direction.</p>

  <h3>13. Rounding the Altitude Too Early</h3>
  <p>Using 4.6 for <span set:html={rich('[[60/13\\approx4.61538]]')} /> and then computing gives a slightly wrong area, while the exact fraction cancels cleanly to 30. Fix: keep full precision until the final answer.</p>

  <h3>14. Assuming the Hypotenuse Must Be Horizontal</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse can sit at any angle; the formula uses lengths, not orientations. Fix: trust the measurements, not the drawing's rotation.</p>

  <h3>15. Confusing the Altitude Theorem With the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[h^2=pq]]')} /> relates the altitude to the hypotenuse segments; <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> relates the three sides. They answer different questions. Fix: match the theorem to the quantities you have.</p>

  <h3>16. Using an Altitude Measured From a Different Triangle</h3>
  <p>An altitude belongs to one specific triangle. Borrowing 4.8 from a 6-8-10 triangle for a different hypotenuse gives a wrong area. Fix: each calculation uses its own triangle's measurements.</p>

  <h3>17. Assuming the Area Determines Every Side Length</h3>
  <p>Many different right triangles share one area. Knowing <span set:html={rich('[[K=24]]')} /> does not fix <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> individually. Fix: treat area as one equation, not a full description.</p>

  <h3>18. Treating Geometric Area as Exact Material to Purchase</h3>
  <p>The formula gives ideal geometric area. Real projects need waste, overlaps, and cutting allowances on top. Fix: add a project margin after the geometry is done.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8]]')} />, the incorrect calculation skips the halving: <span set:html={rich('[[K=10\\times4.8=48]].')} /> That is the area of the corresponding parallelogram. The correct calculation is <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24}]]]')} /> A second illustration: substituting leg 8 for the altitude gives <span set:html={rich('[[(10\\times8)/2=40]]')} />, wrong because 8 is not perpendicular to the hypotenuse. The altitude, and only the altitude, pairs with <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Area Calculation Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Seven checks catch the vast majority of bad results before they leave the page.</p>

  <h3>Check 1: Positive Inputs</h3>
  <p>For a valid right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[c>0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h>0]].')} /> Zero or negative lengths describe no triangle.</p>

  <h3>Check 2: The Altitude Limit</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]].')} /> This single inequality rules out the most common impossible input, such as <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[h=6]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Check 3: Confirm the Right Angle</h3>
  <p>The altitude must be perpendicular to the hypotenuse. If the drawing or measurement cannot confirm the 90-degree meeting, the input is not an altitude.</p>

  <h3>Check 4: Square Units</h3>
  <p>Metre inputs give square metres, centimetre inputs give square centimetres. The answer's unit is the square of the input unit, never the input unit itself.</p>

  <h3>Check 5: Cross-Check With the Legs Formula</h3>
  <p>For the 6-8-10 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[(6\\times8)/2=24]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[(10\\times4.8)/2=24]].')} /> Two methods, one triangle, one answer: agreement is the strongest verification available.</p>

  <h3>Check 6: The Geometric Mean Relationship</h3>
  <p>When the hypotenuse segments are known, verify <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=pq]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[4.8^2=6.4\\times3.6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[23.04=23.04]].')} /> A mismatch means the altitude, the segments, or the triangle identification is wrong.</p>

  <h3>Check 7: Compare Against the Maximum Possible Area</h3>
  <p>For a fixed hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[K\\leq c^2/4]].')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, any claimed right-triangle area above 25 square units is impossible. This page makes no claim about the calculator's own validation behaviour; the check is yours to run.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: Method comparison -->
<section id="sec-compare" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Area From Altitude vs. Area From Two Legs</h2>
  <p>Two formulas, one area. <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> needs the two perpendicular legs; <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /> needs the hypotenuse and its altitude. Neither is more correct; they are the same quantity reached from different measurements.</p>
