<!-- Conclusion -->
<section id="sec-conclusion" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>The Hypotenuse and Its Altitude Are All You Need</h2>
  <p>The altitude method is the base-height rule pointed at the longest side. Once the hypotenuse is identified and the perpendicular altitude is in hand, the area is one multiplication and one halving away, with no need to recover the legs first.</p>
  <p>Its real strength is discipline: perpendicularity, the altitude limit, and the geometric mean relationships turn vague triangle sketches into checkable numbers. Run the checks, compare against the legs formula when the legs are available, and the answer earns its trust.</p>
  <p>Keep this page's calculator for the cases where the hypotenuse and altitude are the measured data, and reach for the legs, angle, or semiperimeter calculators when the measurements point elsewhere.</p>
</section>
